CRITERION 4 FAIL — dual-formatter split confirmed live, not just in the Go
source. C4's own text: "shows the exact saved value ... per the existing
display formatter — no new formatter". Attempt 2's diff
(.swarm/diffs/WS1.2.diff:1677, 1718, 1755) changes the QA/slider display
spans in web/templates/components/whatif/healthcare-person.html FROM
`${{formatNumber .CurrentMonthlyCost}}` TO `{{formatDollars
.CurrentMonthlyCost}}` (and the same for .MedicareMonthlyCost,
.ACACostAfterEmployer) — literally the "new formatter" C4 prohibits — and
wires aria-valuetext to the same new formatDollars call, so span==aria
without depending on JS to settle. formatNumber is Go %.0f (round-half-to-
even); formatDollars/formatWholeDollars is int64(v+0.5) (round-half-up) —
they disagree on exact .50 ties.

The SAME underlying fields are ALSO rendered a few lines further down in
the SAME file's "whatif-healthcare-timeline" template block ("Coverage
Timeline"), which attempt 2 left untouched: still
`${{formatNumber .CurrentMonthlyCost}}` / `${{formatNumber
.MedicareMonthlyCost}}` / `${{formatNumber .ACACostAfterEmployer}}`, plain
text with no `data-quick-adjust-display` attribute — confirmed by grep of
web/static/js/*.js that no JS selector (getQuickAdjustDisplays filters on
`[data-quick-adjust-display]`; the Coverage Timeline spans have no such
attribute) ever touches this block, on load or after any htmx swap. This
is not a sub-100ms pre-hydration artifact (attempt 1's non-surviving
finding) — it is the PERMANENT, final, server-rendered and never-corrected
state.

Rendered proof, same page, same load, same stored figures (GET /whatif on
my temp server, raw HTML, no JS involved for either side):
- current_monthly_cost=1800.50 (healthcare person 9166cc49...):
  - Quick Adjust / in-card display span AND aria-valuetext (line ~638,
    ~5511, ~645): "$1,801"
  - Coverage Timeline, same person, ~90 lines later in the same document
    (line ~772): "Employer $1,800"
- medicare_monthly_cost=2150.50, same person:
  - Quick Adjust / in-card display span AND aria-valuetext (line ~711,
    ~5574): "$2,151"
  - Coverage Timeline (line ~775): "Medicare $2,150"

A user with the employer-coverage timeline visible (a normal, non-edge
configuration: any employer-coverage person with EmployerCoverageYears>0)
sees two different dollar amounts for the one stored monthly cost on the
SAME screen, simultaneously, permanently — not a race, not a pre-hydration
gap. A screen-reader user gets a third: aria-valuetext says "$1,801" while
the adjacent plain-text timeline (not itself an ARIA construct, but the
same rendered page) says "$1,800".

This is the exact defect class the task brief names verbatim ("does any
OTHER surface render the same value with the old formatter now ...
Coverage Timeline ... test exact .50 values like 1654.50") and the
constitution's "dual formatters" recurring-defect class (SPEC.md
Recurring defect classes #3). It is not pre-existing: on base 643fa54 the
QA/slider span ALSO used formatNumber, so base has NO divergence between
the span and the Coverage Timeline (both formatNumber, consistent);
attempt 2's own edit introduced the split, in the very file (manifest
entry web/templates/components/whatif/healthcare-person.html) this attempt
touched. Squarely in-scope and worker-fixable without exceeding the task
(e.g. give the Coverage Timeline spans a `data-quick-adjust-display`
hook, or keep formatNumber on the QA/slider span and only add
aria-valuetext via a formatNumber-consistent computation, or add a
permanent test asserting the Coverage Timeline text and the QA span render
the identical string for the same stored value).

